{"test_id": "B06-001", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "Why does CCoP 2.0 require complex passwords rather than just long passwords?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Character diversity increases search space exponentially", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.2.2"}, {"fact": "Blocks common dictionary-based attacks", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.2.2"}, {"fact": "Balances usability with security strength", "tier": "supporting", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.2.2"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 Section 5.2.2 regarding password complexity - preventing brute force is: Prevent brute force and dictionary attacks by increasing entropy.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Character diversity increases search space exponentially (critical)\n- Blocks common dictionary-based attacks (critical)\n- Balances usability with security strength (supporting)\n\n\nUnderstanding control intent helps in implementing effective security measures that address the underlying threat rather than merely satisfying checkbox requirements. Organizations should consider both the letter and spirit of each control.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0, 5.2.2", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 5.2.2", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for password complexity - preventing brute force", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.2.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B06-002", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "What is the primary security objective of multi-factor authentication requirements?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Single factor compromise insufficient for access", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.2.3"}, {"fact": "Protects against phishing and credential theft", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.2.3"}, {"fact": "Provides layered authentication defense", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.2.3"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 Section 5.3.1(c) regarding mfa - preventing credential theft impact is: Limit impact of compromised credentials through defense in depth.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Single factor compromise insufficient for access (critical)\n- Protects against phishing and credential theft (critical)\n- Provides layered authentication defense (important)\n\n\nUnderstanding control intent helps in implementing effective security measures that address the underlying threat rather than merely satisfying checkbox requirements. Organizations should consider both the letter and spirit of each control.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §5.3.1", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 5.2.3", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for mfa - preventing credential theft impact", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.1.2", "5.3.1", "5.7.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B06-003", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "Why are there different reporting timelines for critical vs other incidents?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Critical incidents may require cross-sector coordination", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 7.3.1"}, {"fact": "2-hour window enables CSA to activate national response", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 7.3.1"}, {"fact": "Tiered reporting aligns resource allocation with severity", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 7.3.1"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 Section 7.3.1 regarding incident reporting timeline - enabling response is: Enable timely national response to significant threats.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Critical incidents may require cross-sector coordination (critical)\n- 2-hour window enables CSA to activate national response (critical)\n- Tiered reporting aligns resource allocation with severity (important)\n\n\nUnderstanding control intent helps in implementing effective security measures that address the underlying threat rather than merely satisfying checkbox requirements. Organizations should consider both the letter and spirit of each control.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0, 7.3.1", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 7.3.1", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for incident reporting timeline - enabling response", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "7", "clause_reference": ["7.3.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B06-004", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "What is the security purpose of requiring 12-month log retention?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Supports investigation of long-running compromises", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.3.2"}, {"fact": "Enables trend analysis and pattern detection", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.3.2"}, {"fact": "12 months covers typical dwell time detection gap", "tier": "supporting", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.3.2"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 Section 6.3.2 regarding log retention - forensic investigation is: Enable retrospective forensic investigation and threat hunting.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Supports investigation of long-running compromises (critical)\n- Enables trend analysis and pattern detection (important)\n- 12 months covers typical dwell time detection gap (supporting)\n\n\nUnderstanding control intent helps in implementing effective security measures that address the underlying threat rather than merely satisfying checkbox requirements. Organizations should consider both the letter and spirit of each control.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0, 6.3.2", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 6.3.2", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for log retention - forensic investigation", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "6", "clause_reference": ["6.3.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B06-005", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "How does network segmentation contribute to CII security beyond just access control?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Prevents compromise propagation across systems", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.4.1"}, {"fact": "Enables granular security policy enforcement", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.4.1"}, {"fact": "Reduces blast radius of successful attacks", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.4.1"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 Section 5.4.1 regarding network segmentation - blast radius reduction is: Contain breaches and limit lateral movement.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Prevents compromise propagation across systems (critical)\n- Enables granular security policy enforcement (important)\n- Reduces blast radius of successful attacks (critical)\n\n\nUnderstanding control intent helps in implementing effective security measures that address the underlying threat rather than merely satisfying checkbox requirements. Organizations should consider both the letter and spirit of each control.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0, 5.4.1", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 5.4.1", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for network segmentation - blast radius reduction", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.4.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B06-006", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "What security outcome does regular vulnerability scanning aim to achieve?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Identifies known exploitable weaknesses before attackers", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.2.1"}, {"fact": "Prioritizes remediation based on risk", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.2.1"}, {"fact": "Maintains continuous security posture visibility", "tier": "supporting", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.2.1"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 Section 6.2.1 regarding vulnerability scanning - exposure reduction is: Reduce attackable surface by finding and fixing exploitable vulnerabilities.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Identifies known exploitable weaknesses before attackers (critical)\n- Prioritizes remediation based on risk (important)\n- Maintains continuous security posture visibility (supporting)\n\n\nUnderstanding control intent helps in implementing effective security measures that address the underlying threat rather than merely satisfying checkbox requirements. Organizations should consider both the letter and spirit of each control.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0, 6.2.1", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 6.2.1", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for vulnerability scanning - exposure reduction", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security o