{"test_id": "B06-012", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "How does change management contribute to CII security beyond operational stability?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Changes can inadvertently weaken security controls", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.4.1"}, {"fact": "Provides audit trail of configuration modifications", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.4.1"}, {"fact": "Enables rollback of security-impacting changes", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.4.1"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 Section 6.4.1 regarding change management - stability and security is: Prevent security degradation through uncontrolled changes.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Changes can inadvertently weaken security controls (critical)\n- Provides audit trail of configuration modifications (important)\n- Enables rollback of security-impacting changes (critical)\n\n\nUnderstanding control intent helps in implementing effective security measures that address the underlying threat rather than merely satisfying checkbox requirements. Organizations should consider both the letter and spirit of each control.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0, 6.4.1", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 6.4.1", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for change management - stability and security", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security obj