_reference": ["5.2.1", "5.2.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B06-014", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "What is the primary objective of CCoP security monitoring requirements?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Reduces dwell time of undetected compromises", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.3.1"}, {"fact": "Provides visibility into security-relevant events", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.3.1"}, {"fact": "Enables evidence collection for investigation", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.3.1"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 Section 6.3.1 regarding security monitoring - threat detection is: Enable timely detection and response to security incidents.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Reduces dwell time of undetected compromises (critical)\n- Provides visibility into security-relevant events (critical)\n- Enables evidence collection for investigation (important)\n\n\nUnderstanding control intent helps in implementing effective security measures that address the underlying threat rather than merely satisfying checkbox requirements. Organizations should consider both the letter and spirit of each control.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0, 6.3.1", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 6.3.1", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for security monitoring - threat detection", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "6", "clause_reference": ["6.3.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B06-015", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "Why does CCoP emphasize data classification as foundation for data protection?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Not all data requires equal protection", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.5.2"}, {"fact": "Classification drives control selection and resource allocation", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.5.2"}, {"fact": "Prevents over-protection of low-value data and under-protection of critical data", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.5.2"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 Section 5.5.2 regarding data classification - protection proportional to sensitivity is: Enable protection proportionate to data sensitivity and impact.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Not all data requires equal protection (critical)\n- Classification drives control selection and resource allocation (critical)\n- Prevents over-protection of low-value data and under-protection of critical data (important)\n\n\nUnderstanding control intent helps in implementing effective security measures that address the underlying threat rather than merely satisfying checkbox requirements. Organizations should consider both the letter and spirit of each control.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0, 5.5.2", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 5.5.2", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for data classification - protection proportional to sensitivity", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.5.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B06-016", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "What cybersecurity threats does physical security control address?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Prevents unauthorized device insertion", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.7.1"}, {"fact": "Protects against tampering and theft of equipment", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.7.1"}, {"fact": "Limits insider physical access threats", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.7.1"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 Section 5.7.1 regarding physical security - preventing unauthorized physical access is: Prevent direct physical compromise of systems and data.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Prevents unauthorized device insertion (critical)\n- Protects against tampering and theft of equipment (critical)\n- Limits insider physical access threats (important)\n\n\nUnderstanding control intent helps in implementing effective security measures that address the underlying threat rather than merely satisfying checkbox requirements. Organizations should consider both the letter and spirit of each control.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0, 5.7.1", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 5.7.1", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for physical security - preventing unauthorized physical access", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.7.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B06-017", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "What failure mode does a documented incident response plan address?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Ad-hoc response leads to errors and delays", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 7.2.1"}, {"fact": "Pre-defined roles reduce decision paralysis", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 7.2.1"}, {"fact": "Enables coordination across teams and stakeholders", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 7.2.1"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 Section 7.2.1 regarding incident response plan - organized response is: Prevent chaotic and ineffective response to security incidents.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Ad-hoc response leads to errors and delays (critical)\n- Pre-defined roles reduce decision paralysis (important)\n- Enables coordination across teams and stakeholders (critical)\n\n\nUnderstanding control intent helps in implementing effective security measures that address the underlying threat rather than merely satisfying checkbox requirements. Organizations should consider both the letter and spirit of each control.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0, 7.2.1", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 7.2.1", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for incident response plan - organized response", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "7", "clause_reference": ["7.2.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B06-018", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "How does business continuity planning complement cybersecurity controls?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Controls may fail despite best efforts", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 7.4.1"}, {"fact": "BCP provides recovery path when prevention fails", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 7.4.1"}, {"fact": "Addresses availability impact of incidents", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 7.4.1"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 Section 8.2 regarding business continuity - resilience is: Ensure essential functions despite successful cyber attacks.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Controls may fail despite best efforts (critical)\n- BCP provides recovery path when prevention fails (critical)\n- Addresses availability impact of incidents (important)\n\n\nUnderstanding control intent helps in implementing effective security measures that address the underlying threat rather than merely satisfying checkbox requirements. Organizations should consider both the letter and spirit of each control.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §8.2", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 7.4.1", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for business continuity - resilience", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "8", "clause_reference": ["8.2.1", "8.2.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B06-019", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B06", "input": {"question": "What is the security purpose of requiring formal risk assessment for CII operators?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Resources are finite and must be allocated efficiently", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 4.2.1"}, {"fact": "Risk assessment identifies highest-priority vulnerabilities", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 4.2.1"}, {"fact": "Provides basis for control selection and justification", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 4.2.1"}], "expected_response": "The primary security intent of CCoP 2.0 Section 3.2 regarding risk assessment - informed decision making is: Enable security investment decisions based on measured risk.\n\nThis intent is achieved through:\n- Resources are finite and must be allocated efficiently (critical)\n- Risk assessment identifies highest-priority vulnerabilities (critical)\n- Provides basis for control selection and justification (important)\n\n\nUnderstanding control intent helps in implementing effective security measures that address the underlying threat rather than merely satisfying checkbox requirements. Organizations should consider both the letter and spirit of each control.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §3.2", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify the control specified in CCoP 2.0 4.2.1", "Extract the security objective or threat being addressed", "Analyze how the control mechanism achieves its security purpose"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating control purpose incorrectly for risk assessment - informed decision making", "Confusing control mechanism with control intent", "Attributing wrong security objectives to the control", "The security intent or objective of the control", "How the control achieves its security purpose", "The threat or risk the control addresses"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing intent not aligned with CCoP 2.0 principles", "Creating non-existent security objectives"]}, "metadata": {"section": "3", "clause_reference": ["3.2.1", "3.2.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "intent_analysis", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Risk Assessment Guide §3"]}}
